%I A125024 #20 Sep 04 2026 09:19:49
%S A125024 1,2,6,12,18,30,60,90,150,360,540,2250,210,420,630,1050,1260,2100,
%T A125024 2520,3150,3780,5880,9450,12600,15750,30870,75600,105840,126000,
%U A125024 246960,5402250,2310,4620,6930,11550,13860,23100,27720,34650,41580,64680,69300,103950,138600
%N A125024 Triangle read by rows: row n contains the minimal Goedel numbers of unlabeled endofunctions on n points.
%C A125024 The labeled endofunction f : {1, 2, ..., n} -> {1, 2, ..., n} corresponds to the Goedel number prime(1)^f(1) * prime(2)^f(2) * ... * prime(n)^f(n). Then, among all the Goedel numbers for different labeled endofunctions of the same (to isomorphism) unlabeled endofunction, there is a unique minimal Goedel number, which is thus the Goedel number for that unlabeled endofunction.
%C A125024 The length of the n-th row is A001372(n).
%C A125024 Each row begins with primorial n# = A002110(n) corresponding to the endofunction (1, 2, 3, ..., n) -> (1, 1, 1, ..., 1) which maps every integer other than 1 to 1 and loops 1 to itself; and ends with A076954(n) which corresponds to the identity endofunction on n points.
%F A125024 a(A125023(n-1)) = A002110(n).
%e A125024 For example, take the unlabeled endofunction on 4 points which is composed of two 2-cycles. One labeled endofunction (1, 2, 3, 4) -> (2, 1, 4, 3) gives us the Goedel number 2^2 * 3^1 * 5^4 * 7^3 = 2572500. Another labeled endofunction (1, 2, 3, 4) -> (3, 4, 1, 2) gives us the Goedel number 2^3 * 3^4 * 5^1 * 7^2 = 158760. But the minimal Goedel number comes from (1, 2, 3, 4) -> (4, 3, 2, 1) which gives us 75600. Hence 75600 is in row 4.
%e A125024 Table begins:
%e A125024   n | row(n) of Goedel numbers
%e A125024   0 | 1. (formally defining prime(0) = 1)
%e A125024   1 | 2.
%e A125024   2 | 6, 12, 18.
%e A125024   3 | 30, 60, 90, 150, 360, 540, 2250.
%e A125024   4 | 210, 420, 630, 1050, 1260, 2100, 2520, 3150, 3780, 5880, 9450, 12600, 15750, 30870, 75600, 105840, 126000, 246960, 5402250.
%e A125024   5 | 2310, 4620, 6930, 11550, 13860, 23100, 27720, 34650, 41580, 64680, 69300, 103950, 138600, ...
%e A125024   6 | 30030, 60060, 90090, 150150, ...
%e A125024   7 | 510510, 1021020, 1531530, ...
%e A125024   8 | 9699690, 19399380, ...
%o A125024 (PARI) row(n) = {my(goedels=List()); forvec(e=vector(n, i, [1, n]), my(goedel=oo); forperm(n, q, my(r=q*Vecsmall(e)*q^-1); goedel=min(goedel, prod(i=1, n, prime(i)^r[i]))); listput(~goedels, goedel)); Set(goedels)} \\ _Jason Yuen_, Sep 02 2026
%Y A125024 Cf. A001372, A002110, A076954 (Product_{i=1..n} prime(i)^i), A125023 (Number of mappings (or mapping patterns) from k <= n points to themselves; number of endofunctions on k <= n points).
%K A125024 nonn,tabf,changed
%O A125024 0,2
